    <h1>Epena Law: a live operations dashboard for a cross-border law firm</h1>
    <div class="article-meta">
      <span>By Abraham Otieno</span><span>·</span>
      <span>Fractional Chief of Staff</span><span>·</span>
      <span>September 2026</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<article class="article-body">

<p>Epena Law is a corporate and commercial law firm working across the United Kingdom and Cameroon. As its fractional Chief of Staff, I was asked a simple question by the partners: <em>where does every matter stand right now, and who has to act next?</em> Answering it used to take a round of emails. Now it takes one screen.</p>

<h2>At a glance</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Client:</strong> Epena Law (United Kingdom and Cameroon)</li>
  <li><strong>My role:</strong> Fractional Chief of Staff, owning the process design, build and rollout</li>
  <li><strong>Timeline:</strong> brief to live system in under two weeks, September 2026</li>
  <li><strong>Built on:</strong> the firm's own Microsoft 365 (SharePoint, Microsoft Forms, Power Automate, Entra ID sign-in) with a secure web front end on Azure</li>
</ul>

<h2>The problem</h2>
<p>Lawyers were updating matters at different times and in different ways. Partners had no single view of the firm's work, deadlines slipped quietly, and closing a matter did not reliably mean the final documents were filed. Chasing people for updates was a job in itself, and nobody could tell at a glance which files needed a decision.</p>

<h2>What I built</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>One live view of the firm.</strong> Every open matter, its stage, status, next action and who has to act, updated automatically from the lawyers' form answers.</li>
  <li><strong>A simple update habit.</strong> A short Matter Status Form, due every Tuesday and Thursday. Each matter has an "Update on the form" button that opens the form with the lawyer's name, client and matter already filled in, so an update takes minutes.</li>
  <li><strong>Automatic follow-up.</strong> A personal "Your week" email every Friday, a reminder when an update is missed (copied to the partners), and a final Monday recap for the partners only. No manual chasing.</li>
  <li><strong>Documents and requests in one place.</strong> Client files, shared links and document requests, with a prompt to upload final documents whenever a matter closes.</li>
  <li><strong>Partner oversight.</strong> A "My week" view that shows who has filed, who is behind and where next actions are overdue, plus a read-only way to see the dashboard exactly as any team member sees it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Confidentiality by design</h2>
<p>A law firm's matter list is sensitive, so privacy shaped every decision rather than being added at the end.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Only people on the firm's team list can sign in, using their existing work accounts. There are no new passwords.</li>
  <li>Each lawyer sees only their own matters and clients. The partners see the whole firm.</li>
  <li>Everything stays inside the firm's own Microsoft 365. No client data is copied to outside tools.</li>
  <li>The partners' "see it as they see it" view is read-only, so nothing can ever be sent in someone else's name.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How I approached it</h2>
<p>I treated the dashboard as an operating rhythm first and software second. The weekly cadence (update Tuesday and Thursday, reminders Friday, recap Monday) was agreed with the Managing Partner before anything was built. Then every release went through the same discipline: plan it, build it, and verify it on the live system before calling it done. The system checks its own health every weekday, runs a suite of more than 200 automated tests before each release, and has a written knowledge base so the firm is not dependent on any one person to keep it running.</p>

<h2>Early results</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Every matter with a past-due next action became visible on day one, instead of surfacing only when someone asked.</li>
  <li>Status chasing by email was replaced by automatic, scheduled reminders.</li>
  <li>The partners moved from asking "where are we?" to deciding what to do next.</li>
</ul>
<p>The team rollout began in late September 2026. I will add measured outcomes here, such as on-time update rates and overdue matters cleared, after the first full month of use.</p>

<h2>Key learning</h2>
<p>A dashboard only works if the habit behind it is simple. Pre-filling the form, fixing two clear update days and automating the reminders did more for compliance than any chart could. The technology made the habit easy; the rhythm made it stick.</p>

